              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F/N \to G
            which does so; this map is then the one asserted to be an isomorphism.
          %p
            In general it is necessary to proceed otherwise, however, and to give a specific function from a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the set of elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F=\langle X\rangle
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            (generators for its kernel) to produce a presentation of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h2#examples_of_presentations Examples of presentations
          %h3#the_standard_presentation The standard presentation
          %p
            A standard if somewhat trivial example of a presentation is given by the
            %em standard presentation
            of a group,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X= \{x_g\mid g\in G,g\neq 1\}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equal to that set if you like, but sometimes it is better to have a distinct set, for instance, it make for an easier notation for the description of certain morphisms.) The set of relations will be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi g
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi gh
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R =\{x_g.x_h= x_{gh}\mid g,h \in G\},
          %p
            so as a
